# 0020 — memory lifecycle: what the station starts ends with it, and costs as little memory as the host asked for.
# Status: APPLIED by setup-station.sh. Every change is off until its STARNET_* variable is set; company/tools/memory.js
# apply writes them into <root>/.starnet-data/memory.env (the station unit reads it after its own env file).
# Why (2026-11-19, owner: "everything that uses RAM gets a lifecycle"; this machine's audit): a probe's `chrome … &` or
# `sleep … &` outlived the shell.exec call that started it (only a timeout or an abort killed the group); Chrome 157
# moves itself into app-com.google.Chrome-<pid>.scope over the session bus, outside the station's unit, so neither the
# unit's MemoryMax nor stopping the unit reached it (8 orphaned browsers, reparented to systemd --user, found today);
# its com.google.Chrome.* temp dirs piled up in the shared /tmp (2,600 of them, /tmp is RAM here); every concurrent run
# got its own Chrome (about 105 MB idle, 200-500 MB in use) with no cap; a station exiting through process.exit left
# its browsers running.
# What it changes (sidecar/memory-lifecycle.js holds the logic; the hooks in existing files are one or two lines each):
#   STARNET_SHELL_REAP_GROUP=1       shell.exec on this host's POSIX shell: when the command finishes, what is left of its
#                                    process group gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 1.5 s later (shell.js runCommand and
#                                    environment.js runProcess, the production route). Never a command that opens a window
#                                    for the person (opensVisibleWindow); shell.bg (shellbg.js spawns on its own) is not
#                                    touched. The tool description then says to use background:true for long-lived work.
#   STARNET_SHELL_OOM_ADJ=<0-1000>   the command's first line raises its own oom_score_adj (Linux, local backend only), so
#                                    the kernel ends a build before the station (the unit runs at 100).
#   STARNET_BROWSER_OWN_GROUP=1      a launched Chrome leads its own process group (detached), keeps its temp files in
#                                    <profile>/.tmp (emptied at each launch; skipped when the socket path would pass 104
#                                    bytes, where Chrome aborts), and a TEMPORARY headless profile gets
#                                    DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=disabled: so it stays in the station's cgroup. The durable
#                                    station profile keeps the session bus: Chrome holds its cookie key in the desktop
#                                    keyring through it (os_crypt portal), and losing it would sign the station out of
#                                    every site. close() ends the whole group, also a helper that outlived the browser; a
#                                    synchronous process 'exit' handler ends every browser this process launched and has
#                                    not seen exit (gracefulShutdown already closed them on SIGTERM; the proc ledger's
#                                    boot sweep still covers a force-kill).
#   STARNET_BROWSER_LOWMEM=1         --enable-low-end-device-mode --js-flags=--max-old-space-size=256
#                                    --disk-cache-size=33554432 --disable-extensions --disable-background-networking
#                                    --disable-component-update --disable-sync --disable-default-apps --no-first-run and
#                                    Translate,MediaRouter,OptimizationHints merged into one --disable-features, each once.
#   STARNET_BROWSER_RENDERER_LIMIT=n --renderer-process-limit=n. Weakens site isolation: unset by default and no memory
#                                    profile sets it until the risk function has reviewed it.
#   STARNET_BROWSER_IDLE_MIN=n       the station browser closes after n idle minutes (browser-view.js stationIdleMs; a
#                                    visible window keeps its never-idle rule).
#   STARNET_PRIVATE_BROWSERS_MAX=n   at most n per-run browsers at once (the station browser is not counted). The next
#                                    launch waits for a slot, up to 10 minutes, then the tool call fails with "No browser
#                                    is free on this station right now (n in use). Try again later or work without the
#                                    browser."; a run closed while waiting gives its place back and starts nothing.
# Tests: company/patches/tests/0020.test.js (applies this record to a temporary copy of ./starnet, runs StarNet's shell,
# browser-view, browser-close-cleanup, procledger and concurrency tests, then real-process cases for each variable).
# Apply: git -C starnet apply --3way ../company/patches/0020-memory-lifecycle.diff, then node company/tools/memory.js
# apply and scripts/restart-station.sh --when-idle --reason "patch 0020".
# Revert: git -C starnet apply -R ../company/patches/0020-memory-lifecycle.diff
